Introduction to Java:
- It was inspired by C/C++ and similar languages
- It was originally designed for consumer electronics
- It was designed to write once run anywhere (wora)
- Widely adopted accross billions of devices, ranging from smartcards to supercomputer.

How is a Program code produced?
- Human writes code in human readable form (also known as source code)
- It is then converted to machine code(0s and 1s) also known as binary code.
- There are two ways to Achieve this:
1. Compiled: Entire code is translated to machine code in one go in advance
2. Interpreted: Individual instructions translated everytime progaram runs

Producing Machine code:
- Different operating systems (like Linux, Windows, Macos) have different machine codes for same program.

Java is Platform Independent:
- Java can compile the program into byte code of no specific platform in the Java Virtual Machine (JVM)
- Jvm then converts the byte code to machine code in which ever platform it is (linux, windows, mac)
- Jvm is platform specific application, i.e for every OS we have its own Jvm. This makes it easier for
jvm to convert the complied java program to the machine code of any OS.

Let's take an example of a java program Calculator.java
Calculator is a source file, after compilaton it is converted to class file Calculator.class (this is the byte code). This process is called the complilation
Calculator.class is then interepreted by the JVM and translated to the specific machine code.
javac Calculator.java: is for comilation.
java Calculator: is for interpreting the class file to output.
java Calculator.java: is used to directly interpret, but it is time consuming.
